WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.960
The last two decades have marked an era
of ambitious school reform. So, after all

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.180
of that work, our America's schools better?
There is some evidence of improvement.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.570
Over much of that period, the
share of students proficient on state

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:24.390
tests rose one to two percent each year.
Meanwhile, graduation rates skyrocketed.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:30.000
So far, so good. The problem? Test results
and graduation rates are subject to

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.010
manipulation by the same state officials
who take credit for the results. In fact,

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.210
when compared to national data, it turns
out that state-reported test gains

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:45.539
outpaced nationally-reported gains by 50
to over 100 percent. Meanwhile,

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:52.180
international data show that, between
2000 and 2015, US test scores were stagnant,

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.520
as was our international
ranking. In short, the most credible

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.500
numbers show the least evidence of
progress. We can debate why we've seen

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.939
these disappointing results, but
proposals for 2020 and beyond should

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:10.110
start with an honest reckoning. Do you
think America's schools have gotten

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:14.939
better since the year 2000? Let us know
in our poll. Also, let us know what other

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:18.930
topics you'd like our scholars to cover
in 60 seconds and be sure to LIKE and

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:24.140
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

